population so this  trial is innovative in that it  actually randomizes patients to a  therapy in this case this plan for four  cycles which is a dna damage and  chemotherapy or cis platinum plus recap  rib uh  which recap is an experimental part  inhibitor for four cycles followed by  maintenance therapy of vocabulary for 24  weeks  this trial enrolled 135 patients with  enrollment completed in early june with  primary endpoints of one year and  two-year disease-free survival  from this analysis we are obtaining a  variety of biospecimens for corollary  genomic sciences this includes a pre  biopsy pre-treatment prior to neutral  chemotherapy a specimen of surgery and  also normal blood and plasma to perform  correlated genomic sciences using a  variety of sequencing modalities listed  here  we're going to be applying a whole exome  sequencing using ion proton sequencer to  discover germline somatic mutations rna  sequencing to profile this regulated  gene expression and also profiling the  plasma dna to detect circulating tumor  dna as a biomarker of relapse  our goals from the study is to determine  the mutational and expression changes in  the tumor that mediate resistance to  after new adrenal chemotherapy meaning  can we find uh signatures or mutations  that make these tumors resistance in the  first place such that these patients  develop residual disease after new  adjuvant chemotherapy to determine gene  expression signatures and mutated  pathways that predict response to the  experimental arm of cisplatin and  recapyrib  to test germline variation as biomarkers  for treatment response we know  that germline dna variations  particularly how our how enzymes  metabolize drugs  have a play a major role in our response  to both drug efficacy and also to  toxicity  lastly we'll be assessing the  circulating tumor dna as an accurate  biomarker for relapse  one of one of the challenges when we  sequence from clinical trials is that  the samples we get can be very  difficult to work with and can be  challenging as one can imagine many of  these clinical trials are multi-site  trials present across variety of states  and sometimes internationally  executed across various countries and  these samples come in all  variety of uh call it shapes and sizes  that are obtained by the lab such that  sometimes we get very uh good tumors  showing up or left with plenty of tumor  cellularity sometimes we get tumors that  are jet are like on the upper right that  are intertwined with a variety of  stromal cells or sometimes we get tumors  like on the on the bottom here and  center where comprised mostly of stroma  scar tissue and with very little tumor  making and many times almost all samples  being formally fixed perfect embedded  providing an extra challenge for the  study of these samples using next-gen  sequencing  so as we've embarked on sequencing this  clinical trial we performed quite an  extensive quality control analysis to  make sure that we can be successful in  sequencing difficult samples  here we show data where we perform  proton paraffin rna sequencing of the  same sample with a one thousand nanogram  input and also with a very small 50  nanogram input of fragmented art of ffp  rna here we plot the log2 rpkm or the  digital expression values of these  libraries against each other and we  should we demonstrate a very strong  nice correlation between these two  libraries with an r value approaching  0.92  this is very exciting for our standpoint  is that it shows that low input ffp rna  sequencing is feasible from paraffin  tissues at very low inputs  and we've actually replicated this with  other paraffin tissues and shown that  this is actually doable with samples  from a clinical trial  albeit sometimes depending on the age of  the study can affect the  the results of these sort of sequencing  studies but so far we've had very good  uh very good results testing  rna-seq on paraffin tissue from clinical  trials  and with that i'd like to end my talk  and thank just my wonderful  collaborators who made a lot of this  work possible my collaborators here at  the iu school of medicine for medical  oncology dr schneider and george sledge  who just recently moved to stanford  dr sioux clare dr lair ivan nephew lou  and badve and also members of my  laboratory uh jeff solzak ratujatali and  brad hancock along with the wonderful  folks of the susan g komen for the cure  tissue bank who made the study possible  by providing normal breast tissues from  a wonderful repository  available to the public for breast  cancer research  also for those of you who are interested  in a little bit more about what we do my  laboratory loves to maintain a facebook  page  with the research and also some of our  more fun photos and please come see us  there at facebook  and with that i'd like to send it back  to mclean i thank you for your attention  today

